00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:46.520
Welcome to the podcast.

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:47.690
CASSIE KOZYRKOV:
Delighted to be here.

00:03:47.690 --> 00:03:48.800
MELANIE WARRICK: Well,
I'm glad you're here.

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:51.530
So Cassie, I know we've talked
a little bit about this--

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:53.500
what decision intelligence is.

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:55.250
But before we even
get into what that is,

00:03:55.250 --> 00:03:57.420
can you tell us a little
bit about yourself?

00:03:57.420 --> 00:03:58.336
CASSIE KOZYRKOV: Sure.

00:03:58.336 --> 00:04:01.520
I service chief decision
scientist for Google Cloud.

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:04.010
And what I'm really
passionate about

00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:08.620
is data science plus
decision making together.

00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:12.890
And you can think of me as a
sort of reformed statistician

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:16.880
if you will, that's my
core academic background

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:20.779
supplemented with psychology,
economics, and neuroscience.

00:04:20.779 --> 00:04:23.900
And my experience
before this role

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:27.772
was consulting Academia
and data science.

00:04:27.772 --> 00:04:28.730
MELANIE WARRICK: Great.

00:04:28.730 --> 00:04:33.170
So why are you passionate
about decision making?

00:04:33.170 --> 00:04:35.210
CASSIE KOZYRKOV:
Well, see anything we

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:37.990
think we know-- our
knowledge, our insights,

00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:40.580
our impressions they
only really begin

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:43.650
to matter when they
drive our actions.

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:45.680
So decisions are the
way that we interact

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:47.695
with the world around us.

00:04:47.695 --> 00:04:49.070
And while some
people might think

00:04:49.070 --> 00:04:53.450
of decision making as the
way that you meet your goals

00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:55.610
or beat your
competition, there's

00:04:55.610 --> 00:04:58.160
really so much more
to it than that.

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:01.500
Businesses and people who don't
know how to make decisions

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:07.490
well are in danger of having
really large, negative impact

00:05:07.490 --> 00:05:08.580
on the world around them.

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:14.000
So I think of decision making
as a way in which we can build

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:15.560
a better world for all of us.

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:16.810
MELANIE WARRICK: That's great.

00:05:16.810 --> 00:05:19.970
MARK MANDEL: So what exactly
is decision intelligence

00:05:19.970 --> 00:05:20.760
engineering?

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:24.050
CASSIE KOZYRKOV: Well, I like
to think of it as data science

00:05:24.050 --> 00:05:25.880
plus, plus if you will--

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:28.220
well, machine
learning plus, plus.

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:32.270
It focuses on the applied
aspects of data science.

00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:34.850
Making decisions with
data and how to do that.

00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:39.140
Building processes for getting
from the conception of an idea

00:05:39.140 --> 00:05:42.170
to a data science
project running reliably

00:05:42.170 --> 00:05:43.830
and automating decision making.

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:44.330
What

00:05:44.330 --> 00:05:46.040
MELANIE WARRICK:
Is data inspired?

00:05:46.040 --> 00:05:47.180
What does that mean?

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:48.555
CASSIE KOZYRKOV:
Data inspired is

00:05:48.555 --> 00:05:53.450
a sort of joke about
a serious subject.

00:05:53.450 --> 00:05:56.330
It's a term that we
use around Google

00:05:56.330 --> 00:06:00.380
a lot that has to do
with not being data

00:06:00.380 --> 00:06:03.510
driven when you think you are.

00:06:03.510 --> 00:06:05.670
Now, let me give
you an example--

00:06:05.670 --> 00:06:10.835
if I tell you that a hotel has
a star rating of 4.2 and then

00:06:10.835 --> 00:06:14.720
I ask you whether or not you'd
like to stay at that hotel.

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:18.590
I have destroyed your
ability to be data driven.

00:06:18.590 --> 00:06:23.840
You can't use that data, that
4.2, to make your decision.

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:26.570
That's because if
you really wanted

00:06:26.570 --> 00:06:31.120
to stay at this hotel all
along, you will look at that 4.2

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:34.700
and you will say, hey,
that's more than 4.0.

00:06:34.700 --> 00:06:36.580
Sure, I'll go for it.

00:06:36.580 --> 00:06:38.065
Whereas if in your
heart of hearts,

00:06:38.065 --> 00:06:39.440
you really wanted
to keep looking

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:42.180
and go for a different
hotel, you're going to say,

00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:45.200
what am I some kind of animal
to stay at a hotel that's

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:47.500
got less than 4.5 stars?

00:06:47.500 --> 00:06:50.600
And so the way that you
interact with that information

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:52.670
will be driven by
the decision you

00:06:52.670 --> 00:06:55.600
want to make all along,
by your unconscious biases

00:06:55.600 --> 00:06:57.720
and not by the data itself.

00:06:57.720 --> 00:07:02.140
So in order for you
to be data driven,

00:07:02.140 --> 00:07:04.590
the data has to
drive the decision.

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:08.600
And so you have to
have thought about what

00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:12.350
it takes to make you want
to stay at the hotel versus

00:07:12.350 --> 00:07:15.170
not before you see that 4.2.

00:07:15.170 --> 00:07:16.610
Where was your cutoff?

00:07:16.610 --> 00:07:17.990
Was it 4.1?

00:07:17.990 --> 00:07:19.430
Was it 3.5?

00:07:19.430 --> 00:07:23.840
Commit to that early and up
front, then look at the data.

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:25.460
That's data driven.

00:07:25.460 --> 00:07:29.180
Whereas data inspired is where
we, like wales encountering

00:07:29.180 --> 00:07:32.120
plankton, swim around
in some numbers

00:07:32.120 --> 00:07:34.970
and then we reach some emotional
tipping point and we decide.

00:07:34.970 --> 00:07:37.940
And sure, there are numbers
near that decision somewhere.

00:07:37.940 --> 00:07:41.840
It's just not the numbers
that drove the decision.

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:45.890
So that decision would be,
at best, data inspired.

00:07:45.890 --> 00:07:48.380
MARK MANDEL: It sounds
like as you talk through it

00:07:48.380 --> 00:07:51.410
a lot of that is
behavioral sciences as well

00:07:51.410 --> 00:07:52.700
as data sciences.

00:07:52.700 --> 00:07:54.500
Why have those two
really combined here?

00:07:54.500 --> 00:07:56.916
CASSIE KOZYRKOV: Yeah, so I
think of decision intelligence

00:07:56.916 --> 00:07:59.000
engineering as
augmenting data science

00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:01.770
with the behavioral and
managerial sciences.

00:08:01.770 --> 00:08:05.060
And the key here is
that in order for us

00:08:05.060 --> 00:08:07.790
to let the data
drive the decision,

00:08:07.790 --> 00:08:11.360
that decision context has
to be framed up front.

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:14.870
So the decision maker has to
understand what it actually

00:08:14.870 --> 00:08:20.220
takes to get them to want to
do one thing versus another.

00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:23.240
And so that process
where they actually

00:08:23.240 --> 00:08:26.450
think through how they want
to make their decision,

00:08:26.450 --> 00:08:28.430
that process isn't
taught in data science.

00:08:28.430 --> 00:08:30.590
That's not an
engineering process.

00:08:30.590 --> 00:08:34.820
That's taught in behavioral
psychology and in economics.

00:08:34.820 --> 00:08:38.809
That's about incentive
design, cost benefit analysis,

00:08:38.809 --> 00:08:41.210
risk assessment.

00:08:41.210 --> 00:08:45.950
All of the subjects that are not
covered in typical data science

00:08:45.950 --> 00:08:46.735
courses.

00:08:46.735 --> 00:08:48.260
MELANIE WARRICK: They
definitely play a role in--

00:08:48.260 --> 00:08:48.595
CASSIE KOZYRKOV: Absolutely.

00:08:48.595 --> 00:08:50.803
MELANIE WARRICK: --terms of
those types of decisions.

00:08:50.803 --> 00:08:52.610
So for the decision
makers, and I

00:08:52.610 --> 00:08:55.315
know the decision
makers can span

00:08:55.315 --> 00:08:56.690
the company, but
especially those

00:08:56.690 --> 00:08:59.570
who are more on the
leadership side of things--

00:08:59.570 --> 00:09:01.250
how should they
approach decision

00:09:01.250 --> 00:09:03.482
making so it's data driven?

00:09:03.482 --> 00:09:05.690
CASSIE KOZYRKOV: So they
have to frame their decision

00:09:05.690 --> 00:09:10.010
context carefully and
thoughtfully at the beginning.

00:09:10.010 --> 00:09:11.630
So here's where
you want to start--

00:09:11.630 --> 00:09:13.470
let's take that hotel example.

00:09:13.470 --> 00:09:16.730
Your first move is
to think about how

00:09:16.730 --> 00:09:20.000
you would want to make the
decision if you could not

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:21.770
get any more information.

00:09:21.770 --> 00:09:24.650
You know nothing
about the hotel.

00:09:24.650 --> 00:09:27.270
Are you going to stay
in it by default or not?

00:09:27.270 --> 00:09:31.160
So you have to commit to
your default action upfront.

00:09:31.160 --> 00:09:33.620
Your next move then,
and this is where

00:09:33.620 --> 00:09:37.820
it takes a lot of training and
mental flexibility on the part

00:09:37.820 --> 00:09:41.510
of the decision maker, is to
think about what information

00:09:41.510 --> 00:09:43.160
you would want.

00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:46.550
What information you would
use if you could have access

00:09:46.550 --> 00:09:48.830
to everything that's
true about the universe,

00:09:48.830 --> 00:09:50.205
you were all-knowing.

00:09:50.205 --> 00:09:52.370
So you have to think about
framing your decision

00:09:52.370 --> 00:09:55.250
context under full information.

00:09:55.250 --> 00:09:59.600
Would you want to use the
star rating on average?

00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:01.760
Would you want to know
how many bad reviews?

00:10:01.760 --> 00:10:04.580
Or have people written
about bed bugs?

00:10:04.580 --> 00:10:08.060
Or what would you actually
want to know about this hotel

00:10:08.060 --> 00:10:10.520
in order to make your decision?

00:10:10.520 --> 00:10:12.230
And where is your
cutoff-- if you

00:10:12.230 --> 00:10:16.250
are using that average stars
review, if you knew for a fact

00:10:16.250 --> 00:10:18.420
it was 4.1 what would
you do about it?

00:10:18.420 --> 00:10:21.616
If you knew it was for a fact
4.9 what would you do then?

00:10:21.616 --> 00:10:22.490
Where is your cutoff?

00:10:22.490 --> 00:10:24.530
And you don't need
data to do this.

00:10:24.530 --> 00:10:27.650
You need imagination
but it's not easy.

00:10:27.650 --> 00:10:30.500
And then after you've dealt
with full information you

00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:32.450
ask yourself, can I
actually have access

00:10:32.450 --> 00:10:35.600
to this information
I've requested or am

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:39.290
I forced to deal with only
a partial view of reality?

00:10:39.290 --> 00:10:43.790
Not the facts I wanted
but a pale shadow of them.

00:10:43.790 --> 00:10:45.950
If I only have
partial information,

00:10:45.950 --> 00:10:48.180
then I'm making the
decision under uncertainty--

00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:49.960
then it is statistical.

00:10:49.960 --> 00:10:52.970
And then what I need to
do is I need to realize

00:10:52.970 --> 00:10:55.160
that I could make a mistake.

00:10:55.160 --> 00:10:58.750
Uncertainty means well,
mistakes are possible now.

00:10:58.750 --> 00:11:01.970
And I could leave my
default action incorrectly

00:11:01.970 --> 00:11:05.510
but I might also stay on my
default action incorrectly.

00:11:05.510 --> 00:11:07.010
And then I have to
think about how I

00:11:07.010 --> 00:11:09.380
would balance the risks there.

00:11:09.380 --> 00:11:12.410
Which one is worse
and how much worse?

00:11:12.410 --> 00:11:14.360
How much risk am I
willing to accept?

00:11:14.360 --> 00:11:16.940
And only after we've
done all of that can we

00:11:16.940 --> 00:11:19.220
go to a statistician
and have them help

00:11:19.220 --> 00:11:21.770
us move the rest of
the way through making

00:11:21.770 --> 00:11:23.169
that data driven decision.

00:11:23.169 --> 00:11:25.460
MELANIE WARRICK: Data science
I know plays a role here.

00:11:25.460 --> 00:11:27.590
How do you define data science?

00:11:27.590 --> 00:11:28.965
What is that?

00:11:28.965 --> 00:11:30.710
CASSIE KOZYRKOV:
So a lot of folks

00:11:30.710 --> 00:11:33.680
think that the areas
within data science--

00:11:33.680 --> 00:11:36.350
descriptive analytics;
statistical inference,

00:11:36.350 --> 00:11:38.450
or statistics for short,
and machine learning

00:11:38.450 --> 00:11:41.600
and AI are defined by the
algorithms used in them

00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:42.860
but that's not true.

00:11:42.860 --> 00:11:45.740
If you're a creative,
smart data scientist,

00:11:45.740 --> 00:11:49.550
you realize that you can
use any algorithm for any

00:11:49.550 --> 00:11:51.680
of these subdisciplines.

00:11:51.680 --> 00:11:54.830
And so really the
difference between them

00:11:54.830 --> 00:11:57.620
is how they are used
for decision making.

00:11:57.620 --> 00:12:00.050
Data science is just
the umbrella term that

00:12:00.050 --> 00:12:01.450
contains these three things.

00:12:01.450 --> 00:12:03.950
How they're used for decision
making is what separates them.

00:12:03.950 --> 00:12:05.420
So let's have a look.

00:12:05.420 --> 00:12:07.850
If we're making no
decisions, then we're

00:12:07.850 --> 00:12:09.690
doing descriptive analytics.

00:12:09.690 --> 00:12:11.600
Descriptive analytics
is all about asking

00:12:11.600 --> 00:12:14.420
what is here in my
data so that either we

00:12:14.420 --> 00:12:18.910
can make a fact based decision
or we can just get inspired.

00:12:18.910 --> 00:12:22.640
If we are making one or
a few important decisions

00:12:22.640 --> 00:12:24.890
under uncertainty--
decisions with no do overs,

00:12:24.890 --> 00:12:26.570
should we launch this product?

00:12:26.570 --> 00:12:28.580
Yes or no?

00:12:28.580 --> 00:12:31.100
Then that's a
statistical inference,

00:12:31.100 --> 00:12:33.020
statistics for short.

00:12:33.020 --> 00:12:37.100
And if we are stitching
a recipe for making

00:12:37.100 --> 00:12:42.362
a lot of repeated decisions,
then that is machine learning.

00:12:42.362 --> 00:12:45.230
Data science is the umbrella
that connects all three

00:12:45.230 --> 00:12:46.012
of these together.

00:12:46.012 --> 00:12:46.970
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:12:46.970 --> 00:12:50.731
So from a decision
intelligence perspective,

00:12:50.731 --> 00:12:52.730
why should we be excited
about machine learning?

00:12:52.730 --> 00:12:54.080
What does that
bring to the table?

00:12:54.080 --> 00:12:55.580
CASSIE KOZYRKOV:
So machine learning

00:12:55.580 --> 00:12:59.210
is all about algorithmically
creating recipes

00:12:59.210 --> 00:13:00.650
for making decisions.

00:13:00.650 --> 00:13:03.560
And we call those
recipes, "models."

00:13:03.560 --> 00:13:06.740
All that really means is
that machine learning well,

00:13:06.740 --> 00:13:08.550
it's the thing
labeler essentially.

00:13:08.550 --> 00:13:11.070
That's what it is,
to put it bluntly.

00:13:11.070 --> 00:13:12.799
The reason that we
should be excited

00:13:12.799 --> 00:13:14.840
about that approach for
thing labeling as opposed

00:13:14.840 --> 00:13:16.910
to the traditional one
where a programmer has

00:13:16.910 --> 00:13:18.650
to think really hard
about the problem

00:13:18.650 --> 00:13:21.650
and then hand-craft the code
that takes us from the inputs

00:13:21.650 --> 00:13:25.160
to the label that
we want is that some

00:13:25.160 --> 00:13:28.970
tasks it's really,
really hard to come up

00:13:28.970 --> 00:13:30.830
with the instructions for.

00:13:30.830 --> 00:13:34.460
So think about if you see
a picture of an animal

00:13:34.460 --> 00:13:36.560
and you just know
that it's a cat.

00:13:36.560 --> 00:13:39.290
What does your brain
actually do to those pixels

00:13:39.290 --> 00:13:40.760
to get that label?

00:13:40.760 --> 00:13:43.250
It's really hard to
describe what you've done.

00:13:43.250 --> 00:13:46.460
And so how could you possibly
write those instructions

00:13:46.460 --> 00:13:48.500
explicitly for the computer?

00:13:48.500 --> 00:13:52.550
Your only hope is to try
to get that recipe stitched

00:13:52.550 --> 00:13:55.370
by giving the computer a
whole bunch of examples

00:13:55.370 --> 00:13:58.610
and then saying to an algorithm,
a machine learning algorithm,

00:13:58.610 --> 00:13:59.900
figure it out.

00:13:59.900 --> 00:14:02.780
Make that recipe for me
because I can't express it.

00:14:02.780 --> 00:14:05.600
I think of this as
giving us the ability

00:14:05.600 --> 00:14:08.069
to automate the ineffable.

00:14:08.069 --> 00:14:09.860
And that's why we should
really be excited.

00:14:09.860 --> 00:14:12.470
This is a fundamental
leap in human progress.

00:14:12.470 --> 00:14:14.420
This unlocks a
whole class of tasks

00:14:14.420 --> 00:14:15.920
that we couldn't
get computers to do

00:14:15.920 --> 00:14:19.280
because we couldn't explain to
the computer how to do them.

00:14:19.280 --> 00:14:23.360
Now we explained not with
instructions but with examples.

00:14:23.360 --> 00:14:26.150
MELANIE WARRICK: I see
machine learning always spoken

00:14:26.150 --> 00:14:28.700
about in conjunction with
artificial intelligence.

00:14:28.700 --> 00:14:31.160
And I know I have my own
perspective on this space

00:14:31.160 --> 00:14:34.560
but I'd like to hear how you
define artificial intelligence.

00:14:34.560 --> 00:14:36.090
How do you define AI?

00:14:36.090 --> 00:14:38.840
CASSIE KOZYRKOV: So I
see AI typically spoken

00:14:38.840 --> 00:14:41.640
about one of two different ways.

00:14:41.640 --> 00:14:45.080
The first option is to stay
true to how that term has

00:14:45.080 --> 00:14:47.670
been used throughout history.

00:14:47.670 --> 00:14:51.110
And that means staying true to
how it was defined in the '50s.

00:14:51.110 --> 00:14:54.650
And sometimes I like to
joke that that version is

00:14:54.650 --> 00:14:57.500
whatever would have
impressed folks in the '50s.

00:14:57.500 --> 00:15:01.580
And whenever we end up building
that impressive thing--

00:15:01.580 --> 00:15:04.420
maybe our calculators today
might have impressed them.

00:15:04.420 --> 00:15:06.140
But then we just
moved the goalposts

00:15:06.140 --> 00:15:07.410
further and further away.

00:15:07.410 --> 00:15:10.760
So that version is a little
unhelpful for my taste

00:15:10.760 --> 00:15:13.370
because it's both
everything and nothing.

00:15:13.370 --> 00:15:17.280
The other version, what industry
is really excited about--

00:15:17.280 --> 00:15:20.110
everyone wants to
get into AI today.

00:15:20.110 --> 00:15:22.250
That's the version
that I prefer as well.

00:15:22.250 --> 00:15:26.610
And to be more formal, that
version is deep learning.

00:15:26.610 --> 00:15:29.410
That is that part
of machine learning

00:15:29.410 --> 00:15:34.210
where the recipe of the
model could not possibly be

00:15:34.210 --> 00:15:36.760
stitched by a human programmer.

00:15:36.760 --> 00:15:39.430
There is no way
to solve that task

00:15:39.430 --> 00:15:43.250
except to go with examples.

00:15:43.250 --> 00:15:47.020
So a difference and to give
you a quick example here--

00:15:47.020 --> 00:15:48.490
I like wine.

00:15:48.490 --> 00:15:51.910
And I have without
any kind of data

00:15:51.910 --> 00:15:54.130
got a little heuristic
that goes something

00:15:54.130 --> 00:15:56.500
like check the
color of the bottle.

00:15:56.500 --> 00:15:58.130
If it's white don't bother.

00:15:58.130 --> 00:15:59.410
If it's red, let's talk.

00:15:59.410 --> 00:16:01.060
That kind of heuristic.

00:16:01.060 --> 00:16:04.660
And I imagine if I use data
to try to predict whether I'll

00:16:04.660 --> 00:16:07.090
like a new bottle of wine,
use machine learning,

00:16:07.090 --> 00:16:11.680
I might do a little better than
this hacky recipe that I have.

00:16:11.680 --> 00:16:14.380
The point is my recipe does OK.

00:16:14.380 --> 00:16:17.510
I can kind of take
a stab at that task.

00:16:17.510 --> 00:16:19.570
That would be a
machine learning task.

00:16:19.570 --> 00:16:22.990
Whereas this version
of look at the image

00:16:22.990 --> 00:16:25.910
and figure out whether
there's a cat in it or not,

00:16:25.910 --> 00:16:29.770
I have no idea how I would even
start expressing that recipe.

00:16:29.770 --> 00:16:30.940
So that one is AI.

00:16:30.940 --> 00:16:33.610
I would have to use
an algorithm that's

00:16:33.610 --> 00:16:36.700
flexible enough and
sophisticated enough

00:16:36.700 --> 00:16:38.650
to just figure that
out from examples

00:16:38.650 --> 00:16:39.970
even though I can't express it.

00:16:39.970 --> 00:16:41.220
And that would be deep learning.

00:16:41.220 --> 00:16:42.970
MELANIE WARRICK: I like
that you're using recipe.

00:16:42.970 --> 00:16:44.611
Definitely a great
way to express it.

00:16:44.611 --> 00:16:47.110
CASSIE KOZYRKOV: I used to use
model like people in my field

00:16:47.110 --> 00:16:47.680
do.

00:16:47.680 --> 00:16:48.760
Model this, model that.

00:16:48.760 --> 00:16:51.502
And then I got feedback that
not everyone actually knows

00:16:51.502 --> 00:16:52.710
what that word "model" means.

00:16:52.710 --> 00:16:53.440
MELANIE WARRICK: It's true.

00:16:53.440 --> 00:16:55.000
CASSIE KOZYRKOV: But recipe,
I think we can all get behind.

00:16:55.000 --> 00:16:56.374
MARK MANDEL: But
sounds like then

00:16:56.374 --> 00:16:59.560
that AI is more applicable
to decision intelligence

00:16:59.560 --> 00:17:00.900
to ML or--

00:17:00.900 --> 00:17:02.470
like where does
AI fit into this?

00:17:02.470 --> 00:17:05.859
CASSIE KOZYRKOV: So actually
what I prefer for people to do

00:17:05.859 --> 00:17:09.640
is to start with what
task they want to perform,

00:17:09.640 --> 00:17:11.109
if they're going to apply it.

00:17:11.109 --> 00:17:13.750
And it's enough for you to
figure out whether or not

00:17:13.750 --> 00:17:15.849
you need machine learning.

00:17:15.849 --> 00:17:19.750
And if it so happens that
you need the deep learning

00:17:19.750 --> 00:17:23.359
algorithm, you will end
up with an AI application.

00:17:23.359 --> 00:17:26.680
And if you didn't need
it, the right process

00:17:26.680 --> 00:17:29.470
might have you use a much
more lightweight algorithm.

00:17:29.470 --> 00:17:32.890
But you don't need to know in
advance whether it's the AI

00:17:32.890 --> 00:17:33.957
kind of machine learning.

00:17:33.957 --> 00:17:36.040
You just need a good process
for machine learning.

00:17:36.040 --> 00:17:37.390
MELANIE WARRICK: And
then off of that note,

00:17:37.390 --> 00:17:39.098
what are some common
mistakes that you're

00:17:39.098 --> 00:17:42.970
seeing leaders make with AI
or companies make with AI?

00:17:42.970 --> 00:17:45.550
CASSIE KOZYRKOV: When we
talk about machine learning

00:17:45.550 --> 00:17:47.740
I think we do the field a
little bit of a disservice

00:17:47.740 --> 00:17:49.330
because we make it
sound like there's

00:17:49.330 --> 00:17:50.725
only one discipline there.

00:17:50.725 --> 00:17:52.600
When actually there are
two machine learnings

00:17:52.600 --> 00:17:56.010
and they're two completely
different disciplines.

00:17:56.010 --> 00:17:59.830
So maybe we should break them up
into research machine learning

00:17:59.830 --> 00:18:02.440
and applied machine learning.

00:18:02.440 --> 00:18:06.880
And my analogy for this
is the humble microwave.

00:18:06.880 --> 00:18:10.150
If I said that I were
a microwave specialist,

00:18:10.150 --> 00:18:13.630
microwave expert
you might think I'm

00:18:13.630 --> 00:18:15.940
a person who builds microwaves.

00:18:15.940 --> 00:18:18.880
Maybe you'd think I
know how to innovate

00:18:18.880 --> 00:18:20.920
with microwaves in a kitchen--

00:18:20.920 --> 00:18:23.300
build recipes
involving microwaves.

00:18:23.300 --> 00:18:24.890
You might not realize
which one I am.

00:18:24.890 --> 00:18:27.682
So these are the two
different disciplines.

00:18:27.682 --> 00:18:30.265
The research machine learning
is about building the microwaves

00:18:30.265 --> 00:18:33.670
as general purpose tools
for everyone to use.

00:18:33.670 --> 00:18:36.610
And see, you need quite a lot of
education to get into that game

00:18:36.610 --> 00:18:39.917
because how are you going
to build a better algorithm

00:18:39.917 --> 00:18:42.250
or for that matter, a better
microwave than the one that

00:18:42.250 --> 00:18:45.670
exists already if you have
no idea how this one works,

00:18:45.670 --> 00:18:47.960
you've never seen
its wiring diagram?

00:18:47.960 --> 00:18:49.390
So for research
machine learning,

00:18:49.390 --> 00:18:50.800
you need a lot of background.

00:18:50.800 --> 00:18:53.560
It's all about building
those algorithms.

00:18:53.560 --> 00:18:56.100
On the other hand, if you're
going to innovate in recipes

00:18:56.100 --> 00:18:58.990
and actually use these things
at scale in an industrial scale

00:18:58.990 --> 00:19:01.780
kitchen, you need to
know a bunch of things

00:19:01.780 --> 00:19:04.120
but they're not the same as
what you would need in order

00:19:04.120 --> 00:19:05.340
to build the microwave.

00:19:05.340 --> 00:19:06.340
I mean think about this.

00:19:06.340 --> 00:19:09.160
How does a chef know that
the microwave works and is

00:19:09.160 --> 00:19:10.210
what they need?

00:19:10.210 --> 00:19:12.410
Certainly not by reading
the wiring diagram.

00:19:12.410 --> 00:19:15.310
I'm pretty sure that
most chefs don't do that.

00:19:15.310 --> 00:19:18.580
Instead, it's by actually
checking that it works

00:19:18.580 --> 00:19:20.750
and it does what
they want it to do.

00:19:20.750 --> 00:19:25.090
And in addition to having
a microwave that works--

00:19:25.090 --> 00:19:28.690
tasting what it
outputs, they need

00:19:28.690 --> 00:19:30.370
to have thought
about what they want

00:19:30.370 --> 00:19:31.570
to serve to their customers.

00:19:31.570 --> 00:19:34.075
They need to have thought
about their ingredients.

00:19:34.075 --> 00:19:36.130
They need to have thought
about their process

00:19:36.130 --> 00:19:37.900
and how they've done
their taste tests

00:19:37.900 --> 00:19:39.400
and how they run
that whole kitchen.

00:19:39.400 --> 00:19:41.710
So there's a huge
science on that side.

00:19:41.710 --> 00:19:43.720
And the mistake
that leaders make

00:19:43.720 --> 00:19:47.420
is they don't see that there's
a difference between these two.

00:19:47.420 --> 00:19:50.140
And so what they try to do is
they try to build themselves

00:19:50.140 --> 00:19:52.540
a machine learning
team, an AI team.

00:19:52.540 --> 00:19:54.940
And they staff that
entirely with researchers.

00:19:54.940 --> 00:19:57.490
And that is exactly
the same as you

00:19:57.490 --> 00:19:59.350
want to run an
industrial scale kitchen

00:19:59.350 --> 00:20:01.960
and the only people
you hire are the folks

00:20:01.960 --> 00:20:04.060
who've built microwaves
their whole lives.

00:20:04.060 --> 00:20:06.430
Those folks might not know
anything about cooking.

00:20:06.430 --> 00:20:09.800
Might have no empathy
for restaurant customers.

00:20:09.800 --> 00:20:12.622
Now sometimes
businesses luck out.

00:20:12.622 --> 00:20:14.830
They've heard a bunch of
researches and things turned

00:20:14.830 --> 00:20:15.790
out OK.

00:20:15.790 --> 00:20:17.700
That's a happy accident.

00:20:17.700 --> 00:20:20.250
That's because in addition
to the core skills taught

00:20:20.250 --> 00:20:24.450
in a PhD, those
researchers ended up

00:20:24.450 --> 00:20:28.050
developing a love
for the application.

00:20:28.050 --> 00:20:30.420
That's a whole
other set of skills

00:20:30.420 --> 00:20:32.380
that they got by accident.

00:20:32.380 --> 00:20:35.280
And as our site reliability
folks say at Google,

00:20:35.280 --> 00:20:36.870
hope is not a strategy.

00:20:36.870 --> 00:20:39.690
Don't hope that your
researchers will just develop

00:20:39.690 --> 00:20:41.610
that capability out of nowhere.

00:20:41.610 --> 00:20:44.520
Instead, hire for
the applied kitchen.

00:20:44.520 --> 00:20:46.530
Hire for the end-to-end process.

00:20:46.530 --> 00:20:49.140
Hire leaders who
know their business.

00:20:49.140 --> 00:20:52.770
Hire engineers who can
process and visualize

00:20:52.770 --> 00:20:55.410
those ingredients--
that data well for you.

00:20:55.410 --> 00:20:57.600
Hire statisticians who
can do that taste test

00:20:57.600 --> 00:21:00.180
and check that it works
on the other side.

00:21:00.180 --> 00:21:02.790
And maybe you don't even
need the researchers

00:21:02.790 --> 00:21:05.730
because Academia, Google
Cloud, and other places

00:21:05.730 --> 00:21:08.070
like that are building
those microwaves for you.

00:21:08.070 --> 00:21:10.470
So as long as you know
where to go and shop for one

00:21:10.470 --> 00:21:12.515
and take it and try
it out, maybe you

00:21:12.515 --> 00:21:14.640
don't need to reinvent the
wheel-- build that thing

00:21:14.640 --> 00:21:16.390
from scratch yourselves.

00:21:16.390 --> 00:21:21.990
So leaders please don't start
by hiring the researcher only

00:21:21.990 --> 00:21:24.300
and hoping that everything's
going to be great.

00:21:24.300 --> 00:21:26.591
MELANIE WARRICK: I like that
you brought up end-to-end.

00:21:26.591 --> 00:21:28.140
It's a common major
challenge trying

00:21:28.140 --> 00:21:31.320
to figure out how to actually
make the full pipeline work.

00:21:31.320 --> 00:21:34.250
But all these kitchen and food
analogies are making me hungry.

00:21:34.250 --> 00:21:34.890
Mark.

00:21:34.890 --> 00:21:37.560
MARK MANDEL: So we talked a
little about common mistakes.

00:21:37.560 --> 00:21:40.500
But where then should
leaders actually start?

00:21:40.500 --> 00:21:42.180
What's a great starting point?

00:21:42.180 --> 00:21:44.550
CASSIE KOZYRKOV: Before you
even hire any one leaders

00:21:44.550 --> 00:21:45.870
you have a job to do.

00:21:45.870 --> 00:21:47.940
This process should
always start with you.

00:21:47.940 --> 00:21:50.370
And in machine learning
the first thing

00:21:50.370 --> 00:21:54.000
you need to think
about is what outputs

00:21:54.000 --> 00:21:55.260
is your system going to make?

00:21:55.260 --> 00:21:58.050
Machine learning is
essentially a thing labeler.

00:21:58.050 --> 00:21:59.460
So what do you need labeled?

00:21:59.460 --> 00:22:02.670
And if you can't even think
of what you need labeled,

00:22:02.670 --> 00:22:04.380
diving right into
machine learning

00:22:04.380 --> 00:22:05.970
is a little too soon for you.

00:22:05.970 --> 00:22:08.010
Maybe you want to do some
descriptive analytics

00:22:08.010 --> 00:22:12.590
and hopefully we'll get a
chance to chat about that soon.

00:22:12.590 --> 00:22:14.700
But imagine in
this wine example,

00:22:14.700 --> 00:22:17.670
you want to build yourself
a wine classifier.

00:22:17.670 --> 00:22:20.730
Well, what do you want the
system to output for you?

00:22:20.730 --> 00:22:23.460
Maybe I'll like it
or I won't like it

00:22:23.460 --> 00:22:25.260
on a new bottle of wine.

00:22:25.260 --> 00:22:28.920
You have to be able to say
what those outputs should be.

00:22:28.920 --> 00:22:32.430
And then you need to think about
how you would measure success

00:22:32.430 --> 00:22:34.030
and what mistakes are possible.

00:22:34.030 --> 00:22:37.890
And there are really a lot of
options here, decision makers.

00:22:37.890 --> 00:22:40.250
Maybe you're after accuracy.

00:22:40.250 --> 00:22:42.670
Or mistakes are equally bad
as far as you're concerned.

00:22:42.670 --> 00:22:45.510
Missing out on a good
bottle or getting

00:22:45.510 --> 00:22:47.080
a bad bottle, same to you.

00:22:47.080 --> 00:22:51.570
But maybe you are a starving
grad student, I've been there,

00:22:51.570 --> 00:22:53.910
and money is really precious.

00:22:53.910 --> 00:22:57.670
And you're quite happy to
miss out on good wines.

00:22:57.670 --> 00:23:01.380
But if the system
says buy this one,

00:23:01.380 --> 00:23:03.350
you don't want it
to waste your time.

00:23:03.350 --> 00:23:06.080
Well, that's a different
metric from accuracy.

00:23:06.080 --> 00:23:07.830
That one is called precision.

00:23:07.830 --> 00:23:10.500
Maybe on the other hand, you are
now past your graduate school

00:23:10.500 --> 00:23:13.230
years and now you're really
worried about missing out

00:23:13.230 --> 00:23:15.600
on the good, beautiful
wines you would have liked.

00:23:15.600 --> 00:23:17.910
Well, now you're
after recall, which

00:23:17.910 --> 00:23:19.540
is a different thing entirely.

00:23:19.540 --> 00:23:22.560
Of all the beautiful ones
that were there how many did

00:23:22.560 --> 00:23:24.840
it return correctly for me?

00:23:24.840 --> 00:23:27.510
So you have to think about how
you want to measure success.

00:23:27.510 --> 00:23:29.280
And then what you need to do--

00:23:29.280 --> 00:23:31.610
going back to being
data driven, you

00:23:31.610 --> 00:23:34.110
need to make sure that you can
be data driven at your launch

00:23:34.110 --> 00:23:35.490
don't launch
decision at the end.

00:23:35.490 --> 00:23:39.690
So you need to commit to your
minimum performance criterion.

00:23:39.690 --> 00:23:42.720
If you picked accuracy,
where's your minimum cutoff

00:23:42.720 --> 00:23:45.300
for letting the system
see the light of day?

00:23:45.300 --> 00:23:46.710
Is it 80% accuracy?

00:23:46.710 --> 00:23:48.180
Is it 70%?

00:23:48.180 --> 00:23:50.790
But you must commit upfront.

00:23:50.790 --> 00:23:54.480
And what you'll realize,
leaders, you can do all of this

00:23:54.480 --> 00:23:57.720
without any data,
without any employees,

00:23:57.720 --> 00:24:00.360
without any equations
or textbooks.

00:24:00.360 --> 00:24:03.300
This is just you
meditating in a closet.

00:24:03.300 --> 00:24:06.180
And so everyone
who's listening here,

00:24:06.180 --> 00:24:09.630
you can now start thinking about
what would you label if you

00:24:09.630 --> 00:24:11.480
had an awesome thing labeler?

00:24:11.480 --> 00:24:13.680
And so you can all
get started on this.

00:24:13.680 --> 00:24:17.290
And if anyone asks you, are
you doing machine learning?

00:24:17.290 --> 00:24:21.300
You can, with a completely clear
conscience, say, absolutely.

00:24:21.300 --> 00:24:22.080
Yes, I am.

00:24:22.080 --> 00:24:23.730
Because you are
doing the right first

00:24:23.730 --> 00:24:24.897
step in the applied process.

00:24:24.897 --> 00:24:26.313
MELANIE WARRICK:
Off of that note,

00:24:26.313 --> 00:24:29.130
is there any additional advice
that you would have, especially

00:24:29.130 --> 00:24:30.780
for the data science teams?

00:24:30.780 --> 00:24:33.780
CASSIE KOZYRKOV: So I find
myself repeating three words

00:24:33.780 --> 00:24:35.090
over and over.

00:24:35.090 --> 00:24:37.000
It's become a sort of slogan.

00:24:37.000 --> 00:24:40.500
And that is split your data.

00:24:40.500 --> 00:24:44.280
Data splitting is a way
that you can have your cake

00:24:44.280 --> 00:24:46.980
and eat it too, in data science.

00:24:46.980 --> 00:24:48.540
See, when we talked
about data driven

00:24:48.540 --> 00:24:51.990
and we saw that if you've
already seen the data,

00:24:51.990 --> 00:24:55.770
then, at best, your decision
based on that data will be data

00:24:55.770 --> 00:24:58.150
inspired when I should
be driven by the data.

00:24:58.150 --> 00:24:59.940
Because if you use
the data to decide

00:24:59.940 --> 00:25:02.610
what question to ask of
the data well, you know,

00:25:02.610 --> 00:25:03.840
you see what you get.

00:25:03.840 --> 00:25:06.510
The trouble is you can't
use the same dataset

00:25:06.510 --> 00:25:09.840
for both inspiration and
rigorous data driven decision

00:25:09.840 --> 00:25:10.680
making.

00:25:10.680 --> 00:25:12.790
So what's the quick hack?

00:25:12.790 --> 00:25:14.670
Well, you've got one dataset.

00:25:14.670 --> 00:25:17.910
You need more than one
dataset so turn one dataset

00:25:17.910 --> 00:25:19.350
into more than one.

00:25:19.350 --> 00:25:22.740
Into an exploratory
piece and a test piece.

00:25:22.740 --> 00:25:25.245
That lets you have your
cake and eat it too.

00:25:25.245 --> 00:25:28.530
Because now what you do is
you give the exploratory piece

00:25:28.530 --> 00:25:30.930
to everyone in the
organization, no matter

00:25:30.930 --> 00:25:32.400
their education level.

00:25:32.400 --> 00:25:35.010
And everyone can go do some
descriptive analytics on it

00:25:35.010 --> 00:25:37.110
and the 10 second lecture
on descriptive analytics

00:25:37.110 --> 00:25:41.630
is plot all the things
but stay skeptical.

00:25:41.630 --> 00:25:43.945
So you don't need a whole
lot of educational background

00:25:43.945 --> 00:25:45.570
just have a look at
what's in the data.

00:25:45.570 --> 00:25:47.070
And if you can't
look at all of it,

00:25:47.070 --> 00:25:48.510
look at the first five entries.

00:25:48.510 --> 00:25:49.890
That's better than nothing.

00:25:49.890 --> 00:25:51.840
Just get inspired.

00:25:51.840 --> 00:25:54.690
The only mistake you can
make during exploring data

00:25:54.690 --> 00:25:57.580
is taking yourself
too seriously.

00:25:57.580 --> 00:26:00.000
We humans are really
good at finding patterns.

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:02.560
We'll find Elvis in
a piece of toast.

00:26:02.560 --> 00:26:06.240
And we shouldn't
take that seriously.

00:26:06.240 --> 00:26:09.000
And so here you have
your entire organization

00:26:09.000 --> 00:26:12.270
looking for tasty
tidbits of insight

00:26:12.270 --> 00:26:13.500
in this exploratory set.

00:26:13.500 --> 00:26:16.430
What they're sort of
doing is baiting a hook

00:26:16.430 --> 00:26:18.180
that they're going to
fling into the ocean

00:26:18.180 --> 00:26:19.650
where decision makers swim.

00:26:19.650 --> 00:26:21.150
They're going to
try to get decision

00:26:21.150 --> 00:26:23.760
makers to react to
that information

00:26:23.760 --> 00:26:25.980
and make a decision.

00:26:25.980 --> 00:26:28.450
And decision makers
will, for the most part,

00:26:28.450 --> 00:26:31.470
completely ignore that plot
and make their decisions

00:26:31.470 --> 00:26:32.130
some other way.

00:26:32.130 --> 00:26:32.580
That's fine.

00:26:32.580 --> 00:26:33.663
That's par for the course.

00:26:33.663 --> 00:26:37.260
But every now and
then that data insight

00:26:37.260 --> 00:26:41.520
makes the decision maker want
to bite and take an action.

00:26:41.520 --> 00:26:45.050
And if that's not such a
very important decision well,

00:26:45.050 --> 00:26:47.100
you know fling that
fish back into the ocean

00:26:47.100 --> 00:26:48.911
and make the decision
based on inspiration.

00:26:48.911 --> 00:26:49.410
That's fine.

00:26:49.410 --> 00:26:51.600
We're allowed to make
decisions based on intuition

00:26:51.600 --> 00:26:55.060
as long as we're honest
with ourselves about it.

00:26:55.060 --> 00:26:57.930
But if that's a really
important decision,

00:26:57.930 --> 00:26:59.430
then the decision
maker might not

00:26:59.430 --> 00:27:02.940
want to run headlong into
the wall based on Elvis

00:27:02.940 --> 00:27:05.370
found in a slice of toast.

00:27:05.370 --> 00:27:08.130
They want to make sure that
this is something worth taking

00:27:08.130 --> 00:27:08.880
seriously.

00:27:08.880 --> 00:27:11.670
They want to approach
the decision rigorously.

00:27:11.670 --> 00:27:13.650
Well, then they can
use that inspiration

00:27:13.650 --> 00:27:15.272
to frame their decision context.

00:27:15.272 --> 00:27:16.980
And that's really easy
to do because they

00:27:16.980 --> 00:27:19.740
know what they were doing
before the insight came in.

00:27:19.740 --> 00:27:22.360
They know how they thought
about changing their minds

00:27:22.360 --> 00:27:23.360
in response to the data.

00:27:23.360 --> 00:27:25.470
They know what it is
about it that made them

00:27:25.470 --> 00:27:27.100
want to change their minds.

00:27:27.100 --> 00:27:30.660
And then they can go
to the other dataset

00:27:30.660 --> 00:27:35.310
and have their experts check
that it's actually worth

00:27:35.310 --> 00:27:38.250
taking that action, that we
should take it seriously.

00:27:38.250 --> 00:27:41.100
And statistics really is the
science of changing your mind.

00:27:41.100 --> 00:27:43.170
So you'll bring
the statistician in

00:27:43.170 --> 00:27:45.579
and see if it's worth
actually reacting

00:27:45.579 --> 00:27:46.620
to that piece of insight.

00:27:46.620 --> 00:27:47.970
So split your data.

00:27:47.970 --> 00:27:51.220
Now why haven't people
been doing this all along?

00:27:51.220 --> 00:27:53.670
So until fairly recently
it was hard enough

00:27:53.670 --> 00:27:56.620
to scrape together enough data
for one respectably sized data

00:27:56.620 --> 00:27:58.350
set, never mind more than one.

00:27:58.350 --> 00:28:02.310
But now as the world is
generating more and more data,

00:28:02.310 --> 00:28:04.440
most of our listeners
I think have no excuse

00:28:04.440 --> 00:28:05.850
not to split their data.

00:28:05.850 --> 00:28:08.850
You've got so much
don't be stingy.

00:28:08.850 --> 00:28:09.920
Make two datasets.

00:28:09.920 --> 00:28:12.900
Use one for inspiration
and use the other one

00:28:12.900 --> 00:28:14.520
for making decisions well.

00:28:14.520 --> 00:28:17.630
MARK MANDEL: So what would
you say to data scientists who

00:28:17.630 --> 00:28:19.380
are worried about
exploring these datasets

00:28:19.380 --> 00:28:21.930
but they don't necessarily
have that deep educational

00:28:21.930 --> 00:28:22.535
background?

00:28:22.535 --> 00:28:24.410
What do you suggest for
those sort of people?

00:28:24.410 --> 00:28:26.190
CASSIE KOZYRKOV: Well,
look, exploration

00:28:26.190 --> 00:28:28.380
is supposed to be sloppy.

00:28:28.380 --> 00:28:30.170
We don't want to come
to conclusions here.

00:28:30.170 --> 00:28:32.020
We don't want to take
ourselves too seriously.

00:28:32.020 --> 00:28:33.630
Again, we're looking
at that piece of toast

00:28:33.630 --> 00:28:35.650
and we're seeing if we
find Elvis in it or not.

00:28:35.650 --> 00:28:37.680
This isn't the stage
where we get rigorous.

00:28:37.680 --> 00:28:40.550
And so actually I'd
like to rather say

00:28:40.550 --> 00:28:42.480
to data scientists
that think you need

00:28:42.480 --> 00:28:45.630
a deep educational background
here to please calm

00:28:45.630 --> 00:28:49.250
down a little bit and
not bully everyone else.

00:28:49.250 --> 00:28:51.870
The skills that you
need for doing this bit

00:28:51.870 --> 00:28:53.880
are exactly the
same skills that you

00:28:53.880 --> 00:28:55.530
use for looking at photographs.

00:28:55.530 --> 00:28:58.540
And by the way, photographs
are stored as data.

00:28:58.540 --> 00:29:01.350
They're not stored as this
beautiful, visual thing

00:29:01.350 --> 00:29:02.870
that you can make sense of.

00:29:02.870 --> 00:29:05.760
And when you take those numbers
that photographs are stored

00:29:05.760 --> 00:29:08.370
as and you visualize them
with appropriate software--

00:29:08.370 --> 00:29:12.330
I don't know Paint or Google
Photos or what have you,

00:29:12.330 --> 00:29:14.160
then you can begin
to get inspired.

00:29:14.160 --> 00:29:17.234
But you must never take that
photograph too seriously.

00:29:17.234 --> 00:29:18.900
If you just walk down
the street and you

00:29:18.900 --> 00:29:20.910
find some photograph
lying around,

00:29:20.910 --> 00:29:23.460
don't go learning all kinds
of things about the world

00:29:23.460 --> 00:29:24.059
based on it.

00:29:24.059 --> 00:29:26.100
You don't know anything
about where it came from.

00:29:26.100 --> 00:29:29.160
The best that you can do is
say this is what is here.

00:29:29.160 --> 00:29:31.890
And so as long as everyone
has the discipline

00:29:31.890 --> 00:29:34.920
to plot it and stick to
well, what we learned

00:29:34.920 --> 00:29:37.731
is that this is what's here
and maybe we might be inspired.

00:29:37.731 --> 00:29:39.480
We shouldn't take
ourselves too seriously.

00:29:39.480 --> 00:29:41.430
Let's not come to
conclusions until we've

00:29:41.430 --> 00:29:42.870
gone to the other dataset.

00:29:42.870 --> 00:29:44.145
Then it's fine.

00:29:44.145 --> 00:29:46.910
So everyone should be
allowed in and empowered

00:29:46.910 --> 00:29:47.660
to just go for it.

00:29:47.660 --> 00:29:49.101
You already have
the right skills.

00:29:49.101 --> 00:29:50.850
What you're doing with
digital photographs

00:29:50.850 --> 00:29:52.920
it's what you should
be doing with all

00:29:52.920 --> 00:29:54.690
the rest of the datasets.

00:29:54.690 --> 00:29:58.515
The funny thing is though
that data scientists,

00:29:58.515 --> 00:30:01.170
they have a habit many of them--

00:30:01.170 --> 00:30:03.270
I keep saying "they"
I should say "we"

00:30:03.270 --> 00:30:05.220
because I was guilty
of this when I just

00:30:05.220 --> 00:30:08.610
finished my statistics
graduate training.

00:30:08.610 --> 00:30:14.700
We spend a long time in school
getting a gold star every time

00:30:14.700 --> 00:30:19.260
that we use the most
complicated tool in our arsenal.

00:30:19.260 --> 00:30:22.050
And so when we leave
after years and years

00:30:22.050 --> 00:30:25.380
of that reinforcement,
our comfort zone,

00:30:25.380 --> 00:30:28.020
our natural behavior
is to keep doing

00:30:28.020 --> 00:30:30.270
the most sophisticated
thing possible-- the most

00:30:30.270 --> 00:30:32.560
rigorous thing possible.

00:30:32.560 --> 00:30:34.380
And we even kid
ourselves that we

00:30:34.380 --> 00:30:37.470
are saving, rescuing
decision makers in some way

00:30:37.470 --> 00:30:41.340
because decision makers don't
even know that they need this.

00:30:41.340 --> 00:30:44.010
Well, look, in the
exploratory phase,

00:30:44.010 --> 00:30:47.280
taking ourselves too
seriously has no place.

00:30:47.280 --> 00:30:50.820
So don't be rigorous
for its own sake there.

00:30:50.820 --> 00:30:53.460
Take it lightly,
do things quickly.

00:30:53.460 --> 00:30:57.000
And when we are moving
from the exploratory phase

00:30:57.000 --> 00:30:58.770
to the data driven
decision making

00:30:58.770 --> 00:31:01.950
phase in the other dataset,
that should be kicked off

00:31:01.950 --> 00:31:03.470
by the decision maker.

00:31:03.470 --> 00:31:05.280
And the decision
maker is the one

00:31:05.280 --> 00:31:07.750
who's had the training
to think about, well,

00:31:07.750 --> 00:31:08.790
how scared should we be?

00:31:08.790 --> 00:31:09.840
How should we trade risk?

00:31:09.840 --> 00:31:11.070
How important is this?

00:31:11.070 --> 00:31:12.990
How much rigor is required?

00:31:12.990 --> 00:31:15.840
Because they don't teach that
in a data science, computer

00:31:15.840 --> 00:31:18.990
science, statistics
training program.

00:31:18.990 --> 00:31:22.560
So everyone should be
allowed in and don't do rigor

00:31:22.560 --> 00:31:23.910
for its own sake.

00:31:23.910 --> 00:31:25.661
Decision makers should
kick off the rigor.

00:31:25.661 --> 00:31:27.160
MELANIE WARRICK:
Great, I know we're

00:31:27.160 --> 00:31:28.950
getting a little
short on time but I

00:31:28.950 --> 00:31:30.870
wanted to give us
the chance to dive

00:31:30.870 --> 00:31:32.706
in to just a couple things.

00:31:32.706 --> 00:31:34.830
One question that definitely
comes to mind-- people

00:31:34.830 --> 00:31:37.350
are interested in
decision intelligence.

00:31:37.350 --> 00:31:38.980
What are some of the resources?

00:31:38.980 --> 00:31:40.870
Where do you
recommend that they go

00:31:40.870 --> 00:31:42.552
to start exploring this space?

00:31:42.552 --> 00:31:44.760
CASSIE KOZYRKOV: So decision
intelligence engineering

00:31:44.760 --> 00:31:47.250
is a practice that we've
put a lot of effort

00:31:47.250 --> 00:31:49.380
into developing at Google.

00:31:49.380 --> 00:31:52.650
And we've got a lot of training
and resources internally

00:31:52.650 --> 00:31:56.100
but there's not a lot
out there externally.

00:31:56.100 --> 00:31:59.010
When I went about
getting my own training,

00:31:59.010 --> 00:32:03.060
I had to have full
immersion in a whole lot

00:32:03.060 --> 00:32:05.100
of separate degrees.

00:32:05.100 --> 00:32:07.860
I had to study
statistics separately

00:32:07.860 --> 00:32:10.710
from psychology separately
from economics separately

00:32:10.710 --> 00:32:12.330
from neuroscience.

00:32:12.330 --> 00:32:14.310
That's an awful lot
of schooling time.

00:32:14.310 --> 00:32:16.950
Sometimes I think I wonder
about my life choices.

00:32:16.950 --> 00:32:18.360
But we can't expect--

00:32:18.360 --> 00:32:19.610
MELANIE WARRICK: Don't we all?

00:32:19.610 --> 00:32:20.568
CASSIE KOZYRKOV: Right.

00:32:20.568 --> 00:32:24.270
We can't expect everyone out
there who's interested in this

00:32:24.270 --> 00:32:28.020
to go and pursue full
degrees on a whole bunch

00:32:28.020 --> 00:32:29.980
of different disciplines.

00:32:29.980 --> 00:32:31.890
So since there is not
that much out there

00:32:31.890 --> 00:32:34.260
and we've been
developing it in here,

00:32:34.260 --> 00:32:37.710
we've decided to start
sharing a lot of resources

00:32:37.710 --> 00:32:39.370
with the world,
sharing what we know.

00:32:39.370 --> 00:32:40.800
So stay tuned.

00:32:40.800 --> 00:32:41.790
Watch this space.

00:32:41.790 --> 00:32:44.610
There'll be a lot of information
about decision intelligence

00:32:44.610 --> 00:32:45.220
coming soon.

00:32:45.220 --> 00:32:46.180
MARK MANDEL: Follow
you on Twitter?

00:32:46.180 --> 00:32:47.500
CASSIE KOZYRKOV:
Sure, if you like.

00:32:47.500 --> 00:32:49.708
MELANIE WARRICK: Was there
anything else actually you

00:32:49.708 --> 00:32:52.110
did want to say in terms
of decision intelligence

00:32:52.110 --> 00:32:56.350
decision making that we
haven't had a chance to cover,

00:32:56.350 --> 00:32:58.445
especially-- you've had
some wonderful analogies.

00:32:58.445 --> 00:33:00.570
But was there anything else
you wanted to touch on?

00:33:00.570 --> 00:33:01.445
CASSIE KOZYRKOV: Yes.

00:33:01.445 --> 00:33:06.240
I want to talk very briefly
about some challenges in AI.

00:33:06.240 --> 00:33:09.600
When we move from a
research machine learning

00:33:09.600 --> 00:33:12.570
to applied machine
learning as our focus,

00:33:12.570 --> 00:33:14.550
we move from how
does the microwave

00:33:14.550 --> 00:33:17.370
work so we can build
a better one to does

00:33:17.370 --> 00:33:20.490
it work so that I can serve
my customers well and solve

00:33:20.490 --> 00:33:22.140
my business problems?

00:33:22.140 --> 00:33:25.860
Now, "how does it work?"
sounds easy but it isn't.

00:33:25.860 --> 00:33:28.710
Because there are three things
we have to deal with there.

00:33:28.710 --> 00:33:30.490
What does it mean to work?

00:33:30.490 --> 00:33:33.210
What is "it" that
we're trying to do?

00:33:33.210 --> 00:33:35.340
And that is on decision makers.

00:33:35.340 --> 00:33:37.950
Then, the does it work?

00:33:37.950 --> 00:33:40.206
That is on the
statisticians working

00:33:40.206 --> 00:33:41.580
with the decision
makers actually

00:33:41.580 --> 00:33:43.820
making sure that it delivers.

00:33:43.820 --> 00:33:46.200
And the last bit, the
sort of hidden bit,

00:33:46.200 --> 00:33:50.460
is that in any kitchen data
science kitchen as well

00:33:50.460 --> 00:33:53.280
your ingredients matter.

00:33:53.280 --> 00:33:56.070
On what ingredients
does it work?

00:33:56.070 --> 00:33:59.550
If you put bad ingredients,
bad data in you

00:33:59.550 --> 00:34:02.732
shouldn't expect good results
out on the other side.

00:34:02.732 --> 00:34:04.440
What I like to say
about machine learning

00:34:04.440 --> 00:34:07.710
is that the world represented
by your training data

00:34:07.710 --> 00:34:11.909
is the only world that you
can expect to succeed in.

00:34:11.909 --> 00:34:15.270
Think of your training data
little bit like textbooks

00:34:15.270 --> 00:34:17.370
that your student
might study from.

00:34:17.370 --> 00:34:20.080
Maybe you want to teach your
student, I don't know, calculus

00:34:20.080 --> 00:34:22.455
and you've given them a bunch
of textbooks to learn from.

00:34:22.455 --> 00:34:24.974
The examples in
those textbooks--

00:34:24.974 --> 00:34:28.230
the world represented by
them is the only thing

00:34:28.230 --> 00:34:30.300
that student's going to
learn from that process.

00:34:30.300 --> 00:34:32.340
So just because you have
data, just because you

00:34:32.340 --> 00:34:34.060
have textbooks doesn't
mean your students

00:34:34.060 --> 00:34:35.820
are going to learn calculus.

00:34:35.820 --> 00:34:37.949
Open that textbook
and check that what's

00:34:37.949 --> 00:34:40.710
in there is calculus
and not, I don't know,

00:34:40.710 --> 00:34:42.872
Japanese-English
vocabulary pairs.

00:34:42.872 --> 00:34:45.330
Because your students going to
find some patterns in there,

00:34:45.330 --> 00:34:48.750
sure, and stitch a recipe that
connects Japanese with English.

00:34:48.750 --> 00:34:51.389
It just won't be
relevant to your task.

00:34:51.389 --> 00:34:53.230
So your ingredients
really matter.

00:34:53.230 --> 00:34:55.560
So please make sure
that you have thought

00:34:55.560 --> 00:34:59.356
about what "it" is, that you
have checked that it does work

00:34:59.356 --> 00:35:01.980
before you release it, and that
you've thought really carefully

00:35:01.980 --> 00:35:03.510
about your ingredients.

00:35:03.510 --> 00:35:04.710
And split your data.

00:35:04.710 --> 00:35:05.490
