00:35:28.050 --> 00:35:31.500
All right, Mark, how can
I secure my GCP account

00:35:31.500 --> 00:35:33.340
using a YubiKey?

00:35:33.340 --> 00:35:34.959
MARK MANDEL: So I'm
sure many of us--

00:35:34.959 --> 00:35:36.750
or but if you haven't
that's totally fine--

00:35:36.750 --> 00:35:38.900
have heard of say things like
two-factor authentication,

00:35:38.900 --> 00:35:39.250
right?

00:35:39.250 --> 00:35:41.190
So you want to have a password
for something, like a thing

00:35:41.190 --> 00:35:44.010
you know and you want to have
a thing you have like say a--

00:35:44.010 --> 00:35:45.210
MELANIE WARRICK:
Through your email or--

00:35:45.210 --> 00:35:45.720
AOL GREETING VOICE:
You've got mail.

00:35:45.720 --> 00:35:46.860
MELANIE WARRICK:
--sometimes it's a text

00:35:46.860 --> 00:35:47.734
or it's a phone call.

00:35:47.734 --> 00:35:48.360
[PHONE RINGS]

00:35:48.360 --> 00:35:50.580
But it's your password
in combination

00:35:50.580 --> 00:35:51.480
with something else.

00:35:51.480 --> 00:35:51.660
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:35:51.660 --> 00:35:54.000
And so there's also these
devices, like security keys

00:35:54.000 --> 00:35:56.940
or you may have heard YubiKeys,
for physical devices that

00:35:56.940 --> 00:35:58.770
can be used as a
two-factor authentication.

00:35:58.770 --> 00:36:00.060
Basically it's that
thing you have.

00:36:00.060 --> 00:36:00.990
MELANIE WARRICK: Some of
them are shaped almost

00:36:00.990 --> 00:36:01.920
like a thumb drive.

00:36:01.920 --> 00:36:02.380
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:36:02.380 --> 00:36:03.990
MELANIE WARRICK: But they're
not a thumb drive in terms

00:36:03.990 --> 00:36:05.940
of holding actual data on them.

00:36:05.940 --> 00:36:08.280
But they're a way of
proving that you exist

00:36:08.280 --> 00:36:10.742
and that you are next to the
device that you're using.

00:36:10.742 --> 00:36:12.450
MARK MANDEL: What you
can do is basically

00:36:12.450 --> 00:36:15.390
you can secure your Google Cloud
account that gives you access

00:36:15.390 --> 00:36:20.142
to Google Cloud Platform using
all these actual options.

00:36:20.142 --> 00:36:21.600
So you can actually
do it through--

00:36:21.600 --> 00:36:23.730
you can have two-factor
authentication using

00:36:23.730 --> 00:36:26.040
text messages; there's a
Google Authenticator, which

00:36:26.040 --> 00:36:27.780
is a rolling number
that you can use

00:36:27.780 --> 00:36:30.071
as a two-factor authentication
you have on your Android

00:36:30.071 --> 00:36:32.717
and iOS devices; as well as
security keys themselves.

00:36:32.717 --> 00:36:34.800
And we'll have a link in
the show notes describing

00:36:34.800 --> 00:36:35.850
how you can do that.

00:36:35.850 --> 00:36:40.280
But what ends up happening is
when you say do GCloud auth

00:36:40.280 --> 00:36:42.390
login, so you're doing
a GCloud authentication

00:36:42.390 --> 00:36:44.220
or you're trying to do
something through the console,

00:36:44.220 --> 00:36:45.600
it asks you for
your two-factor auth

00:36:45.600 --> 00:36:47.610
when you log in so that
your authentication has

00:36:47.610 --> 00:36:48.910
that extra layer of security.

00:36:48.910 --> 00:36:50.910
MELANIE WARRICK: Yes, and
YubiKeys in particular

00:36:50.910 --> 00:36:52.620
are up and coming in
the security field.

00:36:52.620 --> 00:36:55.890
So seen as more
secure in comparison

00:36:55.890 --> 00:36:57.060
to like receiving a text.

00:36:57.060 --> 00:36:58.830
So if you have the
opportunity, use that

00:36:58.830 --> 00:37:01.440
and I know you can use that
on a number of different sites

00:37:01.440 --> 00:37:04.650
that you can log in to,
like GitHub for example.

00:37:04.650 --> 00:37:05.580
You should explore it.

00:37:05.580 --> 00:37:08.542
Your Gmail-- but definitely look
at that for the Google Cloud.

00:37:08.542 --> 00:37:10.500
Something else we wanted
